<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Publius Cornelius Scipio (consul 218 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 211 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Publius Cornelius Scipio was a Roman consul of 218 BCE and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>. After failing to stop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley, he was defeated and wounded at the Ticinus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He then joined his brother Gnaeus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where from their base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> for several years until both were defeated and killed in 211 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tarraco itself had been the base of the brothers Gnaeus and <b>Publius Cornelius Scipio</b> from 218 BCE, and over the next two centuries Roman control spread inland from the Mediterranean coast through the Celtiberian Wars, which ended with the destruction of Numantia in 133 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
